  "slug": "inflammation-crossroads-a8ea",
  "summary": "Single-cell and spatial profiling now resolves immune-stromal interactions driving fibrosis across heart, intestine and skeletal muscle.",
  "synthesis": "Across the items, the dominant thread is immune-stromal crosstalk in fibrotic and remodeling tissue. In Crohn's intestinal fibrosis, CD47hi fibroblasts and SIRPAhi macrophages are linked to IL-1β-associated phagocytic evasion, while in CTX-injured skeletal muscle, expanded M4-like macrophages drive fibroblast activation through an MMP12/SPP1/NF-κB/TNF-α axis and suppress myogenesis. Cardiac studies extend this theme to efferocytic and macrophage programs: a curated cardiac immune atlas reports MERTK-dependent macrophage shifts, and myeloid Gpnmb deletion worsens pressure-overload dysfunction through VEGF-independent angiogenesis. Neutrophil extracellular traps are implicated in intraplaque angiogenesis after myocardial infarction. The LMNA organoid, chemotherapy response eQTL, and miR-208b-3p studies address cardiomyocyte and fibrotic responses more indirectly. The novelty lies mainly in resolving these interactions at single-cell and spatial resolution.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-25"
}
